-- Count Inversions
INSERT INTO problems (slug, title, description, constraints, difficulty, time_limit_ms, memory_limit_mb, published) VALUES ($q$count-inversions$q$, $q$Count Inversions$q$, $q$An **inversion** in an array `a` is a pair of indices `i < j` with `a[i] > a[j]`. Count the inversions of the given array.

A naive double loop is `O(n^2)` and will be too slow for the largest inputs; think divide and conquer (merge sort) or a Fenwick tree.

### Input
- The first line contains `n`.
- The second line contains `n` space-separated integers.

### Output
Print the number of inversions. The answer can exceed 32 bits.$q$, $q$- `1 <= n <= 2 * 10^5`
- `-10^9 <= a[i] <= 10^9`$q$, $q$HARD$q$, 2000, 256, TRUE) ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING;
